djotls is a [`Djot`](https://djot.net) toolkit written in Rust, with a
parser, formatter (`djotfmt`) and language server (`djotls`).

## Build

``` bash
cargo build --release
```

## Install

``` bash
cargo install --path .
```

## Usage

You can check the usage by running:

``` bash
cargo run -r -- --help
```

Or after installing:

``` bash
djotfmt --help
djotls --help
```

Formatting escapes literal syntax punctuation in ordinary text so line
wrapping preserves its meaning. Links, attributes and code keep their
own syntax.

Inline code and math preserve spaces, tabs and line endings. Long spans
can exceed the configured line width.

Use `djotfmt --check file.dj` to check formatting without changing
files.
The exit status is 0 when formatted, 1 when formatting is needed, and 2
for an execution error. Multiple inputs and `-c` work in check mode;
`--check` cannot be combined with `-i`. With no input, it checks
standard
input. Paths needing formatting are reported on standard error.

## Language server

The `djotls` executable provides formatting, navigation, references,
heading outlines, completion and diagnostics over stdio. See the
[language server guide](docs/lsp.dj) for setup and scope.

## Contributing

See the [contribution guide](CONTRIBUTING.dj) for test prerequisites,
documentation updates and release procedures. The [Djot syntax
reference](third_party/djot/doc/syntax.md) is included through the
upstream submodule.

